---
import Container from "@/components/container.astro";
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy"
  description="How the Lob iPhone app and Mac app handle the text you send, Bluetooth, the camera, Accessibility, shortcuts, and purchases.">
  <Container>
    <article class="max-w-3xl pt-16 pb-24 md:pt-12">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold lg:text-5xl lg:tracking-tight">Privacy</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-500">Updated November 24, 2026</p>
      <div class="mt-8 space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        <p>
          Lob is an iPhone app and a Mac menu bar app. Text you enter on the
          iPhone goes over Bluetooth to your Mac and is typed at the cursor.
          Lob has no accounts. The developer does not receive that text.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The text you send</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        <li>
          The iPhone app sends the text in the box. Dictation uses the voice
          input already on your iPhone. Lob does not request the microphone,
          and it receives the finished text only.
        </li>
        <li>
          The text travels over Bluetooth to the Mac you paired by scanning its
          code.
        </li>
        <li>
          The Mac app types it into the field that has focus. It holds the text
          in memory only while typing, and does not write it to a file.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">iPhone</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        <li>Bluetooth is used to connect to your Mac and send text.</li>
        <li>
          The camera is used only to scan the pairing code on your Mac. Lob
          keeps the code so it can reconnect, and does not keep the picture.
        </li>
        <li>
          Shortcuts you create, and the setting for pressing Return on the Mac,
          stay on this iPhone.
        </li>
        <li>Forgetting the Mac deletes the saved pairing code.</li>
        <li>
          When the iPhone connects, it proves to the Mac that it is the Lob
          app, using Apple App Attest. The private key stays on the iPhone.
          Apple issues the first proof for a Mac, and that request does not
          include your text. The Mac keeps the matching public key on that Mac
          and uses it to recognize this phone later.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Mac</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        <li>
          Bluetooth is used to receive text from your iPhone. While Lob is
          running, the Mac advertises a short code from its own identifier, so
          your iPhone can tell it apart from another Mac running Lob.
        </li>
        <li>
          Accessibility is used to type the received text at the cursor. With
          "Press Return on Mac" on, it also presses Return. Lob does not read
          the screen, other apps, or keys you type on the Mac keyboard.
        </li>
        <li>
          The pairing code is shown on the Mac. Its secret is new each time the
          Mac app launches, so an old code stops working. The Mac keeps a
          stable identifier so your iPhone can recognize it.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Purchases</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        Text Shortcuts on the iPhone unlock with a yearly subscription or a
        one-time purchase. Apple processes the payment. Lob checks the unlock
        on the device. Payment details stay with Apple. A subscription is
        managed in Settings → Apple ID → Subscriptions.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What Lob leaves alone</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        <li>
          Lob does not track you across apps or websites. The iPhone app's
          privacy report lists no tracking and no collected data types.
        </li>
        <li>There is no analytics or advertising SDK.</li>
        <li>
          Lob does not collect contacts, location, photos, or the contents of
          other apps.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">This website</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
        These pages are static. The site does not ask you to sign in, and it
        does not use tracking cookies.
      </p>
    </article>
  </Container>
</Layout>
